>> >
>> > Red book says:
>> >
>> > =A0(It is possible to have fu'ivla like "spa'i=94 that are five
>> > letters =A0long, but they must have "=A0'=A0=94; no gismu contains
>> > "=A0'=A0=94.)
>>
>> I seem to recall "spa'i" was a typo for "spra'i", since " ' "
>> didn't count as a letter in that context.
>
> Well, then the text would have to be:
>
> =A0(It is possible to have fu'ivla like "srpa'i=94 that are six letters
> =A0=A0long, but they must have "=A0'=A0=94; no gismu contains "=A0'=A0=94=
.)
>
> which seems pretty pointless, since 6 letters isn't like a gismu
> anyway.

If " ' " doesn't count as a letter, then "spra'i" has five letters. In
other words, "spra'i" has CCCVV form, and this is a valid fu'ivla
form, as long as the vowels are separated by " ' " so you can have
penultimate stress.

> Is there a version of this worth inserting back in, or should I just
> not bother?

There are several other valid five-letter fu'ivla forms: CCVVV, VCCVV,
VCCCV and VVCCV, and those don't need apostrophe, so I would omit the
comment.

